course: biology
lesson: 329
title: Tortoises, lizards and geckos as pets: heat, light and washing hands
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Tortoises, lizards and geckos are reptiles, and reptiles cannot make their own body heat. They warm up and cool down by moving between sunny and shady spots, so a pet tank must copy the outdoors with a warm end and a cool end.
explain: They also need sunshine of a special kind. Reptile lamps give UVB light, which helps the body make vitamin D to use calcium; without it bones grow soft and bent, and window glass blocks most UVB.
explain: Picture a crested gecko licking its own eyeball: it has no moving eyelids, so its tongue is its windscreen wiper. Reptiles are full of such tricks, from dropping tails to sticky toes that walk up glass.
explain: Healthy reptiles often carry salmonella germs, so everyone washes their hands with soap after holding them, and small children under five should not handle them.
fact: Reptiles cannot make their own body heat, so a tank needs a warm end and a cool end, and a cold reptile cannot digest its food properly.
fact: Reptile lamps give UVB light like sunshine, which helps the body make vitamin D; without it reptiles cannot use calcium and their bones grow soft and bent, and window glass blocks most UVB.
fact: Many reptiles carry salmonella germs without being ill, so hands are washed with soap after touching them, and children under five should not handle reptiles.
fact: Geckos can drop their tail to escape a predator; a new tail grows back shorter and fatter, and because the tail stores fat, losing it is a big loss.
fact: Most geckos have no moving eyelids and clean their eyes with their tongue, but leopard geckos are unusual geckos with eyelids that can blink.
fact: Pet tortoises often live 50 years and some much longer, and Jonathan, a giant tortoise on St Helena, is reported to be around 190 years old.
fact: Tortoises from cooler places slow right down and sleep through winter, and a vet checks them first because a sick or thin tortoise can die during it.
fact: A bearded dragon bobbing its head is signalling that it is in charge, waving one arm is a calm giving-way signal, and its beard turns dark when stressed or showing off; bearded dragons come from the dry lands of Australia.
fact: A crested gecko has five toes on each foot with wide pads covered in millions of tiny hairs that grip glass very closely, so it can walk up walls and upside down; leopard geckos have no sticky pads.
fact: Lizards shed their skin in pieces while snakes usually shed in one long piece, many geckos eat their old skin, and a damp hide helps a gecko shed so it does not lose toes.
fact: A tortoise cannot leave its shell, because its ribs and backbone are joined to it as part of its skeleton, and it can feel touch through the shell.
exercise: Why does a tortoise sit under a heat lamp? | Reptiles cannot make their own body heat | It is drying its shell off after a long bath | It is trying to get a nice brown suntan on its shell | The lamp scares away the insects it hates
exercise: Why does a reptile tank need a cool end too? | So the animal can move to the right temperature | Reptiles prefer to be cold all day and only warm up at night | To keep its food frozen | So it can sleep in snow
exercise: What happens to a reptile that is too cold? | It cannot digest its food properly | It starts to make its own heat, like a mouse | It grows a much thicker, heavier shell | It gets faster, livelier and much hungrier
exercise: What does a UVB lamp help a reptile make? | Vitamin D, so it can use calcium | New bright colours for the skin on its back | Venom for its bite | Its eggs in a few days
exercise: Why is a sunny windowsill not enough for a lizard? | Window glass blocks most UVB | Windows make the light too bright | Lizards hate seeing outside | Sunlight is bad for all reptiles
exercise: Why should you wash your hands after holding a tortoise? | Reptiles often carry salmonella germs without being ill | To stop the tortoise catching a cold from your hands next time | Tortoise shells are covered in glue | Tortoises leave poisonous paint
exercise: Who should not handle reptiles because of germs? | Small children under five | Teenagers over thirteen | Only grandparents | Nobody, reptile germs are always harmless
exercise: Why can a gecko drop its tail? | To escape a predator that grabs it | Because it is too heavy to carry | To give it to its babies | To swap it for a longer one
exercise: What is a gecko's tail used for besides balance? | Storing fat | Breathing under water | Laying eggs in the sand | Hearing sounds
exercise: How do most geckos clean their eyes? | They lick them with their tongue | They rub them clean on the glass of the tank | They blink very fast | They never clean their eyes
exercise: Which gecko has eyelids and can blink? | The leopard gecko | The crested gecko | All geckos | No gecko at all
exercise: About how old is Jonathan, the giant tortoise on St Helena, reported to be? | Around 190 years | Only around 19 years | Around 900 years or more | Only around 50 years
exercise: Why does a vet check a tortoise before it sleeps through winter? | A sick or thin tortoise can die during the winter sleep | Tortoises need a winter passport stamped by the vet before they can sleep | Tortoises grow too fat in winter | The vet paints its shell for winter
exercise: What does a bearded dragon mean when it waves one arm? | It is calmly giving way | It wants to start a fight | It is saying hello to people | It is asking for some food
exercise: How can a crested gecko walk up glass? | Millions of tiny hairs on its toe pads grip very closely | It spreads a sticky glue from its mouth onto its feet as it climbs | It has suckers like a squid | It has hooks that dig into glass
exercise: How do lizards usually shed their skin? | In pieces | In one long piece like a snake | Never, lizards keep the same skin | Only once in their whole life
exercise: Why can a tortoise never climb out of its shell? | Its ribs and backbone are joined to the shell | The shell only comes off in summer, when the weather is warm | The shell is glued on by mud | It is too lazy to get out
test: In a cartoon a tortoise climbs out of its shell to take a bath. What is wrong? | The shell is part of its skeleton, so it can never leave it | Nothing, tortoises bathe without their shells | Only old tortoises can leave their shells | Tortoises leave their shells only at night
test: Count them: a crested gecko has five toes on each foot. How many toes on all four feet? | 20 | 16 | 18 | 10
test: Count them: 2 tortoises and 1 bearded dragon sit in the sun. How many legs and tails? | 12 legs and 3 tails | 12 legs and 1 tail | 6 legs and 3 tails | 8 legs and 3 tails
test: «grabbed the gecko by the tail and it came off, it's broken now» What is true? | Geckos drop tails to escape; it will regrow shorter, but never pick one up by the tail | It is broken for ever and the gecko will die soon, because geckos need their tails to live | The tail will grow back exactly the same tomorrow | Geckos like having their tails pulled
test: «tank's right next to the window, sun's out, don't need that lamp» What is the truth? | Window glass blocks most UVB, so the lizard still needs its lamp | Window sun is even better than the lamp, because it is real sunlight | Lizards do not need light at all | The lamp is only for heat in winter
test: «tortoise looks fine, why wash hands, it's clean» What is the best answer? | Healthy reptiles can carry salmonella germs that make people ill | Only dirty tortoises carry germs, so a clean one is safe to hold | Tortoises are too clean to carry germs | It is just to keep the tortoise clean
test: What would happen to a lizard kept for months with no UVB? | Its bones could grow soft and bent | It would grow an extra pair of legs | It would become faster and stronger | Nothing at all would change in its body
test: In Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles the turtles walk on two legs and fight with their hands. What is true of real tortoises and turtles? | They walk on four legs, and the shell is part of their skeleton | They walk upright like people when nobody watches | They can unzip their shells and step out | They use their front legs only as arms
test: «my gecko just licked its own eye, gross, is it ill» What is the best answer? | No, most geckos have no moving eyelids and clean their eyes with their tongue | Yes, it means the gecko is very sick and should go to the vet straight away | Yes, it is trying to eat its eye | It means the gecko is hungry for eyes
test: «grandpa had this tortoise as a kid and now I've got it» How can that be? | Pet tortoises often live 50 years or much longer | Tortoises are reborn every few years | It must be a different tortoise | Tortoises stop the clock while asleep
test: Why does damp air help a gecko shed its skin? | Damp skin peels off more easily, so none gets stuck on its toes | Water soaks into the old skin and dissolves it all away at once | Damp air makes the skin grow faster | Damp air makes geckos sleep
exam: Identify the reason for which a reptile kept in captivity requires a source of external heat. | Reptiles are incapable of generating their own body heat | Reptiles are prone to catching colds | The heat serves to harden the shell | The heat prevents the shedding of skin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the function of ultraviolet lamps in reptile enclosures. | They enable the synthesis of vitamin D and thereby the use of calcium in the bones | They serve exclusively to warm the enclosure and have no further effect upon the animal's health | They improve the colouration of the skin | They are a decorative addition without effect upon health
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between a tortoise and its shell? | The shell forms part of the skeleton, being joined to the ribs and backbone | The shell is a separate covering that may be removed at will | The shell is shed and replaced annually | The shell is formed from the skin of other animals
exam: Ascertain the reason for which hands ought to be washed after the handling of a reptile. | Reptiles frequently carry salmonella bacteria without themselves being ill | Reptiles secrete a poison through their scales which is absorbed through the skin | Reptiles transmit the common cold to human beings | Reptiles leave a dye upon the hands
